The model can't read images. I need another way to verify. Options:
1. Use a JS-based verification: run the page in headless chromium and dump console diagnostics. I can create a test harness that loads the page and, after a delay, evaluates key facts about the scene (positions, opacities, geometry) and prints them via console.log, which headless shell prints to stdout (we saw console messages with --enable-logging? Actually the GL messages appeared because headless shell prints console by default with `--enable-logging=stderr`? They appeared in output, so console messages get printed. Let me use `--enable-logging=stderr --v=0` and add a test script that console.log's verification data.

Approach: create a temp copy of the HTML with an injected test script at the end that:
- waits for load + a few frames
- logs: THREE version, camera position, number of parts, assembled positions, opacities (should be 1), then triggers explode via keydown event, waits ~4.5s (setTimeout), logs exploded positions, opacities (should be targets), label visibility, dashed line visibility/opacity, shutter opacity (should be opaque material with no transparency).

Since the page's own functions are global (declared with function/let at top level of a classic script — they're in global scope: animState, parts, labels, dashedLines, animState are `let` at top level → accessible in global scope via window? Top-level `let`/`const` are NOT properties of window but ARE accessible in the global lexical scope from other scripts ✓ — an injected inline script in the same page can reference them directly ✓.

Also do pixel-level checks: read pixels from WebGL canvas via toDataURL? For transparency check of shutter — hard. Simpler: assert material properties: shutter mesh material transparent === false ✓.

Also can do a bounding-box check: project key world points to NDC to confirm they're on-screen and roughly centered.

Also verify no JS errors: listen to window.onerror and log.

Let me write the test harness. Also verify exploded visual coverage: for each part, check position equals expected; opacity values; labels div opacity transitions to 1 (style.opacity set to 1 after showLabels — check some label's computed opacity ~1 after 4s real... with virtual time budget it fast-forwards; but console.log timing via real timers — in the harness I'll use setTimeout with real time and a long virtual-time-budget so everything settles.

Note: with --virtual-time-budget, timers fast-forward. The keydown dispatch after 'load' — fine.

Actually simpler: use real time: headless shell screenshot waits for virtual time budget; console logs appear as they happen. I'll set virtual-time-budget=15000 and the harness uses setTimeout chains (which fast-forward under virtual time). The explosion animation uses requestAnimationFrame + clock.getDelta() — getDelta under virtual time: THREE.Clock uses performance.now() — under virtual time, does performance.now() advance? Virtual time budget affects timers and rAF; performance.now might also be virtualized (it is, in Chrome's virtual time). If rAF fires with virtual timestamps, delta accumulates properly → animation completes ✓.

Let me write the harness:

```html
<script>
window.__errors = [];
window.addEventListener('error', e => console.log('PAGE-ERROR: ' + e.message));
// wait for scene ready
setTimeout(() => {
  const log = (...a) => console.log('CHECK: ' + a.join(' '));
  log('three', THREE.REVISION);
  log('camera', camera.position.x, camera.position.y, camera.position.z);
  log('state', animState);
  parts.forEach(p => log('part', p.name, 'pos', p.group.position.y.toFixed(2),
    p.mats.length ? 'op=' + p.mats[0].opacity.toFixed(2) : 'opaque'));
  // shutter material check
  // find shutter meshes
  const shutter = parts.find(p => p.name === 'SHUTTER');
  shutter.group.traverse(o => { if (o.isMesh) log('shutter-mat', o.material.transparent, o.material.opacity, o.material.color.getHexString()); });
  // assembled positions
  // trigger explode
  document.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent('keydown', { code: 'Space' }));
  log('state-after-trigger', animState);
  setTimeout(() => {
    log('state-final', animState);
    parts.forEach(p => log('part-final', p.name, 'pos', p.group.position.x.toFixed(1), p.group.position.y.toFixed(1), p.group.position.z.toFixed(1),
      p.mats.length ? 'op=' + p.mats[0].opacity.toFixed(2) : 'opaque'));
    log('dashed-visible', dashedLines.map(l => l.visible).join(','), 'dashed-op', dashedLines[0].material.opacity.toFixed(2));
    log('labels', labels.map(l => l.div.style.opacity).join(','));
    // geometry checks: channel hole exists in top/bottom shell shapes? check via HEAD_CHANNEL values
    log('channel', JSON.stringify(HEAD_CHANNEL));
    // window local coords on shutter plate: find the extrude geometry hole
    // count error listeners
    log('errors', window.__errors.length);
  }, 12000);
}, 1500);
</script>
```

Wait — accessing `parts`, `labels` etc from injected script: they're top-level `let` in the main script — the injected script is a separate classic script; top-level let bindings live in the global lexical environment shared across scripts ✓ accessible ✓.

Under virtual time 15000: the initial 1500ms wait + 12000ms wait + inner animation — total ~13.5s < 15s ✓.

But — do the CSS2D labels' opacity transitions matter? style.opacity set to '1' — check string ✓.

Also check that during assembled state the parts' Y match: top 1.5 etc.

Also verify NDC projection of a few points to make sure things are on screen: I can add in the harness: project (0,46,0) and (-32,-15,32) → NDC. Log them. But since I can't see images, NDC bounds check is a decent proxy: expect x,y within [-1,1] — actually the full exploded stack: y from -16.5 (dashed) to 48.5 (labels) — ortho frustum height 90 at zoom 1 — camera looks at target (0,15,0) after explosion: NDC y range ±45 around target... the stack center 15 ± 45 → -30..60 covers -16.5..48.5 ✓.

Also I want to verify pixel output isn't blank: read canvas via `renderer.domElement.toDataURL().length` > some threshold, or use gl.readPixels count of non-transparent pixels. Simpler: toDataURL length > 5000.

Let me also add a check for the top shell channel hole being a real hole: the base extrude geometry: count vertices... complex. Instead, verify via shape: I can't easily introspect. But the harness CAN access the created shape? Not stored. Skip — geometry was constructed from the same HEAD_CHANNEL; the visual check via... hmm. Let me add a quick geometric probe: raycast? Overkill. The code review covers it.

